1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Listen to a talk in a psychology class.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,433
Cognitive dissonance refers to the mental discomfort experienced

3
00:00:09,473 --> 00:00:15,230
when a person holds two or more contradictory beliefs, values or attitudes simultaneously

4
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:19,757
This theory, developed by psychologist Leon Festinger in the 1950s,

5
00:00:19,757 --> 00:00:27,000
suggests that people are motivated to reduce this discomfort by changing their attitudes, beliefs or behaviors.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,200
For example, imagine someone who values health but smokes cigarettes.

7
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:35,340
The conflict between their behavior (smoking) and their belief

8
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:38,270
valuing health) creates cognitive dissonance.

9
00:00:38,270 --> 00:00:42,680
To reduce this discomfort, they might decide to quit smoking

10
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:47,280
or rationalize their behavior by downplaying the health risks associated with smoking.

11
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:52,080
Cognitive dissonance can also play a significant role in decision making.

12
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:54,400
When faced with a difficult choice,

13
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:57,400
people often experience dissonance after making a decision

14
00:00:57,640 --> 00:01:00,200
particularly if the chosen option has downsides.

15
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:05,051
To alleviate this discomfort, they may seek out information that supports their choice

16
00:01:05,051 --> 00:01:07,290
and ignore information that contradicts it.

17
00:01:07,290 --> 00:01:10,409
Understanding cognitive dissonance is crucial for psychologists

18
00:01:10,409 --> 00:01:13,973
because it explains various aspects of human behavior

19
00:01:13,973 --> 00:01:20,210
including why people sometimes act in ways that contradict their stated beliefs and values.

20
00:01:20,210 --> 00:01:25,257
Next, we will discuss some experiments conducted to study cognitive dissonance

21
00:01:25,257 --> 00:01:28,010
and their implications for psychological research.
